    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the diagnostic evaluation for abusive head trauma, the significance of each finding, and the differential diagnosis.',
      content: '**Classic Triad of Abusive Head Trauma (AHT)**:\n\n1. **Subdural Hematomas (SDH)**:\n   - Bilateral SDH of different ages is **highly specific** for AHT — indicates repeated episodes of trauma\n   - Mechanism: Rotational acceleration-deceleration forces cause tearing of bridging veins between the dural surface and brain\n   - The reported mechanism (2-foot fall from couch onto carpet) is **biomechanically inconsistent** with this injury pattern. Short falls from <3 feet rarely cause any intracranial injury and virtually never cause bilateral SDH.\n\n2. **Retinal Hemorrhages**:\n   - Bilateral, multilayered, extending to the periphery (ora serrata) — this pattern is **almost pathognomonic** for AHT\n   - Mild, isolated retinal hemorrhages can occur from birth trauma, CPR, or accidental head injury — but extensive multilayered hemorrhages to the periphery are seen almost exclusively in abusive trauma\n   - Mechanism: Vitreoretinal traction from repetitive acceleration-deceleration forces\n\n3. **Encephalopathy/Diffuse Brain Injury**:\n   - MRI showing diffuse axonal injury with hypoxic-ischemic changes indicates severe, widespread brain damage\n   - Mechanism: Shearing of axons from rotational forces plus secondary hypoxic-ischemic injury from apnea and cerebral edema\n\n**Additional Evidence of Non-Accidental Trauma**:\n- **Healing posterior rib fractures**: Classic metaphyseal fractures from squeezing the chest — these are considered **highly specific** for abuse. They require significant force and are not seen in normal handling or CPR.\n- **Metaphyseal corner fracture** (bucket-handle/corner fracture) of distal tibia: Another **highly specific** fracture for abuse — caused by twisting/pulling forces on extremities\n- **Multiple fractures of different ages**: Indicates repeated episodes of trauma over weeks\n- **Fingertip pattern bruising**: On upper arms and flank — consistent with forceful gripping\n- **Growth faltering**: Weight dropped from 50th to 25th percentile — may indicate neglect or chronic stress\n\n**Essential Workup**:\n1. **Skeletal survey** (completed) — AP and lateral views of all long bones, hands, feet, skull, spine, ribs, pelvis. Repeat in 2 weeks to detect healing fractures not visible acutely.\n2. **Ophthalmology consultation** (completed) — dilated fundoscopic examination with documentation\n3. **MRI brain** (completed) — more sensitive than CT for diffuse axonal injury, parenchymal contusions, and dating of injuries\n4. **Laboratory studies**: CBC with differential, coagulation panel (PT, PTT, INR, fibrinogen), Factor VIII and IX levels, Von Willebrand panel — to rule out bleeding diathesis. Also metabolic panel, hepatic panel (screening for occult abdominal trauma), urinalysis\n5. **Continuous EEG monitoring** (completed) — for seizure detection\n\n**Differential Diagnosis to Exclude**:\n- **Bleeding disorders**: Factor deficiencies, Von Willebrand disease — must be ruled out with lab work\n- **Glutaric aciduria type I**: Metabolic condition causing macrocephaly and SDH — screen with urine organic acids\n- **Osteogenesis imperfecta**: Can cause multiple fractures — but does NOT cause SDH or retinal hemorrhages. Blue sclerae, family history, and collagen studies can differentiate.\n- **Benign enlargement of subarachnoid spaces**: Can predispose to SDH from minor trauma but does not explain retinal hemorrhages, fractures, or bruising pattern\n- **Birth trauma**: Only relevant in the immediate neonatal period, not at 8 months',
