      content: '**Neurodevelopmental Monitoring & Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Prognosis**: This infant has sustained severe brain injury with diffuse axonal injury and hypoxic-ischemic damage. The prognosis is guarded. Expected long-term outcomes may include:\n   - Cerebral palsy (spastic quadriparesis pattern based on current examination)\n   - Intellectual disability\n   - Cortical visual impairment (CVI)\n   - Epilepsy\n   - Feeding difficulties\n   - Communication impairment\n\n2. **Acute Rehabilitation Interventions** (inpatient):\n   - **Physical therapy**: Positioning program to counteract extensor posturing. Gentle ROM to prevent contractures. Supported upright positioning to promote head control. Parent training in handling techniques.\n   - **Occupational therapy**: Sensory stimulation program (visual, auditory, tactile). Hand splinting to address fisting and prevent contractures. Feeding therapy — assess oral motor function and coordinate with speech therapy.\n   - **Speech therapy**: Swallow evaluation (modified barium swallow or FEES if indicated given coughing with feeds). Oral motor stimulation. Early communication assessment.\n   - **Tone management**: Serial monitoring of tone. Consider oral baclofen if tone significantly interferes with positioning, care, or comfort. Avoid botulinum toxin in infants this young.\n\n3. **Early Intervention (EI) Referral**:\n   - **Federal mandate** under IDEA Part C: Infants and toddlers (0-3 years) with established developmental delays or conditions with high probability of delay are entitled to early intervention services\n   - This infant qualifies automatically based on the diagnosis of brain injury with documented developmental regression\n   - **EI services include**: Developmental therapy, PT, OT, speech therapy, vision services, family training and support — all provided in the natural environment (typically the home)\n   - Referral should be made **prior to hospital discharge** — the EI evaluation must occur within 45 days of referral\n   - Develop an **Individualized Family Service Plan (IFSP)** — the family-centered plan of care under Part C\n\n4. **Seizure Management**:\n   - Continue levetiracetam 20 mg/kg/day divided BID\n   - Target seizure freedom — if breakthrough seizures occur, titrate to 40-60 mg/kg/day\n   - If levetiracetam fails, second-line options for infantile epilepsy include: oxcarbazepine, phenobarbital (less preferred due to cognitive effects but sometimes necessary in refractory infantile seizures), or lacosamide\n   - Monitor for infantile spasms — this infant is in the age range (4-12 months) and the brain injury is a major risk factor. If clusters of brief flexion or extension spasms develop, obtain urgent EEG to evaluate for hypsarrhythmia. Infantile spasms require ACTH or vigabatrin as first-line treatment.\n   - **EEG follow-up**: Routine EEG at 1 month and 3 months post-injury, then as clinically indicated\n   - Continue seizure precautions: supervised sleep positioning, no unsupervised bathing\n\n5. **Vision Services**:\n   - Cortical visual impairment (CVI) assessment and grading (Roman-Lantzy CVI Range)\n   - Vision stimulation program: high-contrast toys, controlled lighting, preferred color identification (often red/yellow in CVI)\n   - Referral to teacher of the visually impaired through EI\n\n6. **Follow-Up Schedule**:\n   - Pediatric physiatrist: Monthly for the first 6 months, then quarterly\n   - Neurology/epilepsy: Monthly until seizures controlled, then every 3 months\n   - Ophthalmology: Every 3 months for CVI monitoring\n   - Developmental pediatrician: Every 6 months for comprehensive developmental assessment\n   - Repeat neuroimaging (MRI) at 6 months to assess for evolving encephalomalacia and hydrocephalus',
