      content: '**Mandatory Reporting of Child Abuse**:\n\n1. **Legal Obligation**: All healthcare providers are **mandated reporters** in all 50 states. The duty to report arises when there is **reasonable suspicion** of abuse — not certainty. Failure to report is a criminal offense (misdemeanor in most states, felony in some).\n\n2. **What to Report**:\n   - Report to CPS (Child Protective Services) and/or law enforcement per state-specific protocols\n   - The report should include: child\'s identifying information, nature and extent of injuries, discrepancy between reported mechanism and injury pattern, other indicators of abuse (prior injuries, growth faltering)\n   - **You do not need to determine who committed the abuse** — that is the role of CPS and law enforcement\n\n3. **Documentation Standards**:\n   - Thorough, objective medical documentation is essential and may be used in criminal proceedings\n   - Document injuries with **precise descriptions**: size, shape, color, location, pattern. Use body diagrams.\n   - **Photograph** all injuries with a ruler for scale (hospital protocol)\n   - Document the reported mechanism and the medical opinion regarding biomechanical plausibility\n   - Avoid using conclusory language like "child abuse" in isolation — instead: "The constellation of findings (bilateral SDH of different ages, extensive retinal hemorrhages, healing rib fractures, and metaphyseal fractures) is consistent with abusive head trauma and is not explained by the reported mechanism of a short fall."\n\n4. **CPS Coordination During Hospitalization**:\n   - Attend CPS case conferences as the medical expert\n   - Provide written medical summary for the court if requested\n   - Coordinate with the child abuse pediatrician (CAP) — the medical specialist in forensic child abuse evaluation\n   - Advocate for the child\'s medical needs in custody and placement decisions\n\n**Foster Care Medical Home Model**:\n- If this infant is placed in foster care, he will need a **medical home** — a single coordinating primary care provider who manages all medical, developmental, and specialty care\n- The AAP recommends that children in foster care receive:\n  - Comprehensive medical evaluation within 72 hours of placement\n  - Developmental screening at every visit\n  - Mental health screening (relevant for older children)\n  - Coordination of specialty care appointments\n  - A portable medical record that travels with the child through placements\n- **Challenge**: Children in foster care experience fragmented care due to placement changes, Medicaid transitions, and multiple caseworkers. The physiatrist should ensure the rehabilitation plan is clearly documented and portable.\n- All children in foster care are eligible for **Medicaid** automatically — ensuring coverage for rehabilitation services\n\n**Advocacy Role of the Physiatrist**:\n- Provide clear documentation of the child\'s medical and functional needs to inform custody decisions\n- Recommend that any placement (mother, family member, or foster care) must be able to meet the child\'s complex medical needs: seizure management, therapy attendance, follow-up appointments, feeding safety\n- If the mother retains custody, recommend a safety plan and parenting support services\n- Testify as an expert witness if subpoenaed regarding the mechanism and extent of injuries',
