tigation. Those actions will speak louder than any words.\'\n\n**Key Principles in This Conversation**:\n- **Do not offer opinions on custody or guilt** — the physician\'s role is medical, not legal\n- **Acknowledge the mother\'s distress** without validating or invalidating her claim of ignorance\n- **Maintain clear boundaries**: The child is the patient. The physician\'s advocacy is for the child.\n- **Avoid accusatory language**: Do not say "you should have known" or imply blame. CPS will determine the mother\'s role.\n- **Recognize the mother may be a victim too**: Domestic violence history with the biological father suggests a pattern of abusive relationships. The boyfriend may have been abusive to her as well.\n- **Provide concrete, actionable guidance**: Attend therapy, cooperate with investigation, seek counseling\n- **Document the conversation**: Note the mother\'s statements and emotional state in the medical record objectively, as they may be relevant to the CPS investigation',
